      question: "How do I appeal a tax assessment I disagree with?",
      answer: "**Tax Appeal Process:**\n\n**Tax Appeal Tribunal:**\n\nIf you disagree with a tax assessment, demand notice, or decision by a tax authority, you can appeal to the **Tax Appeal Tribunal**.\n\n**Time Limit:**\n\n• **30 days** from the date of assessment or decision\n• Tribunal may accept late appeals if there's sufficient cause\n\n**Process:**\n\n1. **File appeal** in writing to the Tribunal Secretary\n2. **Pay required fee** (as prescribed)\n3. **Tribunal reviews** your appeal\n4. **Hearing scheduled** (at least 7 days notice)\n5. **Tribunal decides** on the appeal\n\n**What You Can Appeal:**\n\n• Tax assessments\n• Demand notices\n• Decisions by tax authorities\n• Residency disputes\n• Administrative actions\n\n**Appeal Outcome:**\n\n• Tribunal may confirm, reduce, increase, or annul the assessment\n• Decision can be appealed to Federal High Court (on points of law)\n\n**Important:**\n\n• Keep all records and documentation\n• You may need to deposit security (depending on case)\n• You can represent yourself or use a legal practitioner or tax professional\n• Each party bears its own costs\n\n**Note:** The Tribunal provides a fair and independent dispute resolution process for all taxpayers.",
