      question: "What happens if I don't keep proper books of account?",
      answer: "**Record Keeping Requirements:**\n\n**What Records to Keep:**\n\n**Books of Account:**\n\n• **All transactions** - Income and expenses\n• **Sufficient detail** - To correctly ascertain tax liability\n• **English language** - Must be in English (or translated)\n• **Prescribed format** - As required by tax authority\n\n**Required Records:**\n\n• **Income records** - All sources\n• **Expense records** - Business expenses, deductions\n• **Receipts and invoices** - Supporting documents\n• **Bank statements** - All transactions\n• **Digital records** - Electronic documents acceptable\n• **Audited accounts** - For companies (or attested statement for small companies)\n\n**Retention Period:**\n\n• **Minimum 6 years** - After year of assessment\n• **Keep longer** - If audit or investigation ongoing\n\n**Penalties:**\n\n• **Failure to keep books:**\n  - Individuals: ₦10,000\n  - Companies: ₦50,000\n\n• **Failure to provide books:**\n  - ₦200,000 first day\n  - ₦10,000 each subsequent day\n\n**Tax Authority Powers:**\n\n• **Can request** - Books, documents, records\n• **Can access** - Premises, computers, cloud storage\n• **Can remove** - Books temporarily to make copies\n• **Can audit** - At any time\n\n**Important:**\n\n• **Keep everything** - Better too much than too little\n• **Organize records** - Makes audits easier\n• **Digital backup** - Keep electronic copies\n• **English language** - Translate if needed\n\n**Note:** OTax helps you maintain digital records automatically and keeps them organized and accessible.",
